── no DSN
✗ postgres has no connection string

    postgres.Module() was declared without postgres.DSN(...).

  Give it one, usually from config:

      postgres.Module(postgres.DSN(cfg.DatabaseURL))

  Either form works:
      postgres://user:pass@localhost:5432/app?sslmode=disable
      host=localhost user=app dbname=app sslmode=disable

── bad DSN
✗ postgres connection string is not valid

    postgres://app:xxxxx@:not-a-port/app
    context deadline exceeded

  This is a parse failure, not a connection failure — nothing was
  dialled. Check the scheme, the port, and sslmode.

── cannot connect
✗ cannot connect to postgres

    postgres://app:xxxxx@db:5432/app
    context deadline exceeded

  The connection string parsed, so this is the database, the network,
  or the credentials. Check that the server is reachable from here and
  that the role exists — using the variable you set the DSN in, since
  the one printed above has its password removed and would fail auth:

      psql "$DATABASE_URL" -c 'select 1'

── not started
✗ postgres pool used before it was started

    The pool is opened by this module's OnStart hook, at boot step 6.

  Something reached the database during CONSTRUCTION instead. A
  constructor wires; it does not acquire. Move the query into an
  OnStart hook of your own, which runs after this one.

── write outside a transaction
✗ save user outside a transaction

    This write was attempted with no persistence.UnitOfWork in scope.

  It would autocommit — and the events the aggregate raised would stay
  pending on an object about to go out of scope, and be lost. Silently:
  no error, no outbox row, no way to find out afterwards.

  Wrap the use case:

      func (h *handler) Handle(ctx context.Context, cmd Cmd) (Res, error) {
          return res, h.uow.Do(ctx, func(ctx context.Context) error {
              return h.repo.Save(ctx, aggregate)
          })
      }

  or declare the handler with app.Transactional, which does it for you.

  A repository's write does not make this check itself. It calls
  persistence.Write, which makes it — and enlists the aggregate when the
  write succeeds, so its events cannot be left behind.

  Reads need no transaction; only writes are refused.

── table missing
✗ table "warren_outbox" does not exist

    The warren/persistence/postgres module started, but its table has not been created.

  Warren never migrates at boot — under a rolling deploy that races
  every replica and applies DDL the still-serving old ones were not
  written against. Apply the schema as a DEPLOY STEP instead.

  Either run Warren's own applier from a small main, or a deploy job:

      postgres.Migrate(ctx, os.Getenv("DATABASE_URL"), postgres.Schema)

  or point the migration tool you already use at postgres.Schema — the
  files are plain SQL in goose's format, so goose, atlas and dbmate all
  read them unchanged.

── no migrations
✗ no migrations found

    The filesystem passed to Migrate contains no .sql files.

  Pass postgres.Schema for Warren's own tables, or an fs.FS of your
  own numbered files — 00001_name.sql, applied in name order.

── empty migration
✗ migration 00003_add_index.sql has no statements

    Its "-- +goose Up" section is empty.

  A file that applies nothing would still be recorded as applied, and
  the real statements would never run. Write the section, or delete
  the file.

── migration failed
✗ migration 00003_add_index.sql failed

    context deadline exceeded

  It was rolled back and NOT recorded, so fixing the file and running
  again resumes from here — the migrations before it stay applied.
